course: dictionary
lesson: 6577
title: Context clues with a plant word
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word nightshade names a whole family of plants, not just one plant. Some are very poisonous, and some are foods we eat. The words around it are its context, and they show you which kind is meant.
explain: «The berries of deadly nightshade, a wild plant, are poisonous.» The clues are deadly and poisonous. Those words warn you that this plant is dangerous, so nightshade here means a plant that can harm you.
explain: «Potato and tomato plants are relatives of nightshade.» Relatives tells you that nightshade is a family, like a family of people. Put «family of plants» in place of nightshade, and the sentence still makes sense, so you have checked your meaning.
fact: «Nightshade» is a noun meaning a family of plants.
fact: «Deadly nightshade» has shiny black berries, and it is poisonous.
fact: The nightshade family includes foods like potatoes, tomatoes, peppers and aubergines.
fact: «Belladonna» is another name for deadly nightshade, and it means «beautiful lady» in Italian.
fact: Never eat wild berries, because some, like deadly nightshade, are poisonous.
exercise: «The berries of deadly nightshade, a wild plant, are poisonous.» What does poisonous tell you about deadly nightshade? | *Dangerous to eat | Fine to eat if cooked | Sweet and tasty | Good for your eyes
exercise: Which of these is a food from the nightshade family? | Bluebell | Daisy | *Aubergine | Oak
exercise: «Belladonna» means «beautiful lady» in which language? | Spanish | Scots | French | *Italian
test: «Deadly nightshade has tempting shiny black berries.» What does tempting suggest? | *Attractive, so someone might want to eat them | Boring and dull | Ugly and bitter | Hard and green
test: Potatoes turned green by light are kept in the dark. Why? | To make them sweeter | *Because green potatoes carry more poison | To stop them getting wet | Because light makes them grow faster
exam: «The nightshade family also includes foods like potatoes, tomatoes, peppers and aubergines.» What does nightshade mean here? | A kind of sweet | Dark night sky | *A family of plants | A type of bird
